## Kafka Exactly-Once Processing (EOS)

### 1. Idempotent Producer (`enable.idempotence=true`)
Solves network duplicate writes when an ACK from broker to producer drops:
- Broker assigns a unique **Producer ID (PID)**.
- Producer assigns monotonically increasing sequence numbers ($0, 1, 2\dots$) per partition.
- If broker receives sequence number $\le$ last committed, it quietly discards duplicate without duplicating log entry.

### 2. Kafka Transactions (Atomic Read-Process-Write)
Allows publishing output messages AND committing consumer input offsets inside a single atomic transaction:

```java
props.put(ProducerConfig.TRANSACTIONAL_ID_CONFIG, "tx-order-processor-1");
KafkaProducer<String, String> producer = new KafkaProducer<>(props);
producer.initTransactions();

try {
    producer.beginTransaction();

    // 1. Publish processed business event to output topic
    producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("orders-processed", orderId, payload));

    // 2. Commit input consumer partition offsets atomically within same transaction!
    producer.sendOffsetsToTransaction(currentConsumerOffsets, "order-group");

    producer.commitTransaction();
} catch (ProducerFencedException e) {
    producer.close(); // Fenced by another zombie instance
} catch (KafkaException e) {
    producer.abortTransaction(); // Rolls back both offsets and messages!
}
```

### Downstream Consumer Isolation
Downstream consumers must configure:
```properties
isolation.level=read_committed
```
This ensures consumers only read messages from successfully committed transactions, hiding uncommitted or aborted messages behind the **Last Stable Offset (LSO)**.

## Apache Flink: Production State & Fault Tolerance

### Keyed State Primitives
Flink maintains local state per key for high throughput without remote database lookups:

```java
public class TransactionDeduplicator extends KeyedProcessFunction<String, Transaction, Transaction> {
    private transient MapState<String, Long> processedTxns;

    @Override
    public void open(Configuration parameters) {
        MapStateDescriptor<String, Long> descriptor = 
            new MapStateDescriptor<>("tx-state", String.class, Long.class);

        // Auto-expire state to prevent memory bloat:
        StateTtlConfig ttlConfig = StateTtlConfig.newBuilder(Time.hours(24))
            .setUpdateType(StateTtlConfig.UpdateType.OnCreateAndWrite)
            .cleanupFullSnapshot()
            .build();

        descriptor.enableTimeToLive(ttlConfig);
        processedTxns = getRuntimeContext().getMapState(descriptor);
    }

    @Override
    public void processElement(Transaction txn, Context ctx, Collector<Transaction> out) throws Exception {
        if (!processedTxns.contains(txn.getId())) {
            processedTxns.put(txn.getId(), ctx.timestamp());
            out.collect(txn);
        }
    }
}
```

### State Backend Selection

| Feature | HashMapStateBackend | EmbeddedRocksDBStateBackend |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Storage** | JVM Heap memory | Off-heap RocksDB (C++ embedded KV store) |
| **State Size** | Bound by JVM RAM (GBs) | Disk-bound (Terabytes per node) |
| **Checkpoints** | Full state copy | **Incremental checkpoints** (only new SST files) |
| **Speed** | Nanoseconds (in-memory) | Microseconds (deserialization overhead) |

### Checkpointing via Asynchronous Barrier Snapshotting (ABS)
Flink uses a variant of the **Chandy-Lamport algorithm**:
1. JobManager injects **Checkpoint Barriers** into source streams.
2. Barriers flow with event stream through operators.
3. Operator aligns barriers from all inputs (or uses unaligned checkpoints for high backpressure).
4. Operator takes a state snapshot asynchronously and uploads SST files to S3/HDFS without halting stream processing.

## Redis Caching Patterns & Reliability

### Caching Topologies

#### 1. Cache-Aside (Lazy Loading)
Application queries cache first. On miss, reads from DB and populates cache.
```
Read: App ──(1. Check)──► Redis
      │ (2. Miss)
      ▼
     DB ──(3. Return)──► App ──(4. Save with TTL)──► Redis
```

#### 2. Write-Through vs Write-Behind
- **Write-Through**: Application writes to Cache, and Cache synchronously writes to DB before responding. Guarantees consistency at the cost of write latency.
- **Write-Behind (Write-Back)**: Application writes to Cache immediately. Cache batches and asynchronously writes to DB in the background. High write throughput, risk of data loss on cache node crash.

### Cache Stampede (Thundering Herd) & Solutions
When a popular cache key expires, thousands of concurrent requests miss simultaneously, crashing the database.

#### Solution: Distributed Mutex via Redis `SET NX EX`
```java
public Product getProductWithLock(String id) {
    String cacheKey = "product:" + id;
    Product product = redisTemplate.opsForValue().get(cacheKey);
    if (product != null) return product;

    String lockKey = "lock:product:" + id;
    Boolean acquired = redisTemplate.opsForValue().setIfAbsent(lockKey, "locked", Duration.ofSeconds(5));

    if (Boolean.TRUE.equals(acquired)) {
        try {
            product = dbRepository.findById(id).orElseThrow();
            redisTemplate.opsForValue().set(cacheKey, product, Duration.ofMinutes(30));
            return product;
        } finally {
            redisTemplate.delete(lockKey);
        }
    } else {
        // Wait 50ms and retry from cache
        Thread.sleep(50);
        return getProductWithLock(id);
    }
}
```

## Kafka Streams: KStream, KTable & Stateful Processing

### KStream vs KTable

| Concept | KStream | KTable |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Abstraction** | Infinite event stream (each record is an independent event) | Changelog stream (latest value per key = materialized view) |
| **Semantics** | Append — every record is meaningful | Upsert — new record with same key overwrites previous |
| **Analogy** | INSERT-only database log | Current state of a database table |
| **Compaction** | No | Yes (Kafka log compaction keeps only latest per key) |

```java
StreamsBuilder builder = new StreamsBuilder();

// KStream: every payment event matters
KStream<String, Payment> payments = builder.stream("payments");

// KTable: latest account balance per accountId
KTable<String, AccountBalance> balances = builder.table("account-balances",
    Materialized.as("balances-store"));
```

### Join Types in Kafka Streams

#### Stream-Table Join (Enrichment Pattern)
Enrich each event with the current state of a table — no window needed because the table represents current state:

```java
KStream<String, EnrichedPayment> enriched = payments.join(
    balances,
    (payment, balance) -> new EnrichedPayment(payment, balance.getAvailableFunds()),
    Joined.with(Serdes.String(), paymentSerde, balanceSerde)
);
```

#### Stream-Stream Join (Windowed Correlation)
Correlate two event streams within a time window — both sides are unbounded, so a window bounds the join:

```java
KStream<String, ClickEvent> clicks = builder.stream("user-clicks");
KStream<String, PurchaseEvent> purchases = builder.stream("purchases");

KStream<String, ClickPurchaseCorrelation> correlated = clicks.join(
    purchases,
    (click, purchase) -> new ClickPurchaseCorrelation(click, purchase),
    JoinWindows.ofTimeDifferenceWithNoGrace(Duration.ofMinutes(30)),
    StreamJoined.with(Serdes.String(), clickSerde, purchaseSerde)
);
```

### Stateful Aggregations with State Stores

```java
KGroupedStream<String, Payment> groupedPayments = payments
    .groupByKey(Grouped.with(Serdes.String(), paymentSerde));

KTable<Windowed<String>, Long> paymentCounts = groupedPayments
    .windowedBy(TimeWindows.ofSizeWithNoGrace(Duration.ofHours(1)))
    .count(Materialized.<String, Long, WindowStore<Bytes, byte[]>>as("payment-count-store"));

// Query state store interactively (for microservice API)
ReadOnlyWindowStore<String, Long> store = streams.store(
    StoreQueryParameters.fromNameAndType("payment-count-store", QueryableStoreTypes.windowStore())
);
long count = store.fetch("user-123", windowStart, windowEnd);
```

### Topology Description
Always print your topology for debugging:

```java
Topology topology = builder.build();
System.out.println(topology.describe());
// Prints: Source -> Processor -> Sink with state stores
```

### Exactly-Once with Kafka Streams
```java
Properties props = new Properties();
props.put(StreamsConfig.PROCESSING_GUARANTEE_CONFIG, StreamsConfig.EXACTLY_ONCE_V2);
// EXACTLY_ONCE_V2 uses one transaction per task (more efficient than EXACTLY_ONCE)
// Requires: Kafka broker >= 2.5, replication factor >= 3 for changelog topics
```

**EXACTLY_ONCE_V2** introduces per-task transactions (rather than per-thread in v1), reducing the number of open transactions and improving throughput significantly.

### KStream vs Direct Kafka Consumer: When to Choose
- **Kafka Streams**: When you need joins, aggregations, windowing, or interactive queries — it handles state management, fault tolerance, and changelog topics automatically.
- **Raw Kafka Consumer**: Simple event processing, fire-and-forget transformations, or when running inside a framework (Spring Kafka) that provides enough abstraction.

## Dead Letter Topics & Retry Patterns

### Why Dead Letter Topics Are Essential
In Kafka, if a consumer throws an exception, it blocks the partition — that partition's messages pile up until the error is resolved. Without a DLQ strategy, one bad message can halt an entire partition indefinitely.

**Failure causes:**
- Downstream service (DB, API) temporarily unavailable → transient, should retry
- Malformed message / deserialization error → permanent, should DLQ immediately
- Business rule violation → permanent, needs human review

### Spring Kafka @RetryableTopic
Spring Kafka 2.7+ provides first-class retry topic infrastructure:

```java
@Component
public class PaymentConsumer {

    @RetryableTopic(
        attempts = "4",                    // 1 original + 3 retries
        backoff = @Backoff(
            delay = 1000,                  // 1 second initial delay
            multiplier = 2.0,              // Exponential: 1s, 2s, 4s
            maxDelay = 30000               // Cap at 30 seconds
        ),
        exclude = {DeserializationException.class},  // Skip retry for poison pills
        dltTopicSuffix = "-dlt",
        topicSuffixingStrategy = TopicSuffixingStrategy.SUFFIX_WITH_INDEX_VALUE
    )
    @KafkaListener(topics = "payments")
    public void processPayment(PaymentEvent event) {
        paymentService.process(event);
    }

    @DltHandler
    public void handleDlt(PaymentEvent event,
                          @Header(KafkaHeaders.RECEIVED_TOPIC) String topic,
                          @Header(KafkaHeaders.EXCEPTION_MESSAGE) String errorMsg) {
        log.error("DLT received from topic={} error={}", topic, errorMsg);
        dlqRepository.save(new DlqRecord(event, topic, errorMsg));
        alertingService.sendAlert("Payment DLQ entry: " + event.getPaymentId());
    }
}
```

**Auto-created retry topics:**
```
payments
payments-retry-0   (delay: 1s)
payments-retry-1   (delay: 2s)
payments-retry-2   (delay: 4s)
payments-dlt       (dead letter — no more retries)
```

### Fixed Delay vs Exponential Backoff

| Strategy | Config | Use Case |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Fixed Delay** | `delay=5000, multiplier=1` | Simple, predictable retry cadence |
| **Exponential Backoff** | `delay=1000, multiplier=2` | Gradual back-off to reduce downstream pressure |
| **Exponential + Jitter** | Add random jitter to delays | Prevent retry storms from synchronized consumers |

### Poison Pill Detection
A **poison pill** is a message that will *always* fail — malformed bytes, incompatible schema, null required field. Retrying endlessly wastes resources.

```java
@Bean
public DefaultErrorHandler errorHandler(KafkaTemplate<String, Object> template) {
    DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer recoverer = new DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer(template,
        (record, ex) -> {
            if (ex instanceof DeserializationException) {
                // Permanent failure: go straight to DLT, no retries
                return new TopicPartition(record.topic() + "-dlt", record.partition());
            }
            return new TopicPartition(record.topic() + "-retry", record.partition());
        });

    ExponentialBackOffWithMaxRetries backoff = new ExponentialBackOffWithMaxRetries(3);
    backoff.setInitialInterval(1000L);
    backoff.setMultiplier(2.0);

    return new DefaultErrorHandler(recoverer, backoff);
}
```

### DLQ Monitoring & Manual Reprocessing

```bash
# Monitor DLQ size (consumer lag on DLT topic)
kafka-consumer-groups.sh --bootstrap-server broker:9092 \
  --group dlt-monitor --describe

# Manual reprocessing: replay DLT back to original topic
kafka-console-consumer.sh --topic payments-dlt --from-beginning | \
  kafka-console-producer.sh --topic payments
```

**Production DLQ practices:**
1. Alert when DLT has > 0 messages — treat as a high-priority incident
2. Store DLT headers: original topic, partition, offset, exception class and message
3. Build an admin UI to inspect and selectively reprocess DLT records
4. Implement idempotency on consumers so DLT reprocessing is safe

## Apache Flink CEP: Complex Event Pattern Detection

### What Is CEP?
**Complex Event Processing (CEP)** detects meaningful patterns within a stream of events. Classic use cases: fraud detection, intrusion detection, SLA violation alerting, IoT anomaly detection.

FlinkCEP operates on top of the Flink DataStream API and matches stateful patterns across time.

### Pattern API Building Blocks

```java
// Fraud pattern: 3+ failed transactions within 10 minutes for same user
Pattern<TransactionEvent, ?> fraudPattern = Pattern
    .<TransactionEvent>begin("firstFail")
        .where(new SimpleCondition<TransactionEvent>() {
            @Override
            public boolean filter(TransactionEvent event) {
                return event.getStatus().equals("FAILED");
            }
        })
    .followedBy("secondFail")
        .where(SimpleCondition.of(e -> e.getStatus().equals("FAILED")))
    .followedBy("thirdFail")
        .where(SimpleCondition.of(e -> e.getStatus().equals("FAILED")))
    .within(Time.minutes(10));  // All 3 must occur within 10 minute window
```

### Contiguity Strategies

| Method | Contiguity | Description |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `next("B")` | STRICT | B must immediately follow A — no events in between |
| `followedBy("B")` | RELAXED | B follows A, but other events may occur in between |
| `followedByAny("B")` | NON_DETERMINISTIC | Every B after A is matched, even if multiple |

**STRICT** is strictest — use for sequence protocols. **RELAXED** is most common for real-world fraud patterns where other legitimate events may interleave.

### Applying Pattern to Stream

```java
DataStream<TransactionEvent> transactions = env
    .addSource(kafkaSource)
    .keyBy(TransactionEvent::getUserId);  // Match patterns per-user

PatternStream<TransactionEvent> patternStream =
    CEP.pattern(transactions, fraudPattern);

// Side output for timeout (user had only 2 failures — not a match)
OutputTag<Tuple2<String, List<TransactionEvent>>> timeoutTag =
    new OutputTag<>("pattern-timeout"){};

SingleOutputStreamOperator<FraudAlert> alerts = patternStream
    .flatSelect(
        timeoutTag,
        // Timeout handler
        (Map<String, List<TransactionEvent>> pattern, Long timestamp,
         Collector<Tuple2<String, List<TransactionEvent>>> out) -> {
            out.collect(Tuple2.of("TIMEOUT", pattern.get("firstFail")));
        },
        // Match handler
        (Map<String, List<TransactionEvent>> pattern,
         Collector<FraudAlert> out) -> {
            List<TransactionEvent> failures = new ArrayList<>();
            failures.addAll(pattern.get("firstFail"));
            failures.addAll(pattern.get("secondFail"));
            failures.addAll(pattern.get("thirdFail"));
            out.collect(new FraudAlert(failures.get(0).getUserId(), failures));
        }
    );

// Partial matches that timed out
DataStream<Tuple2<String, List<TransactionEvent>>> timeouts =
    alerts.getSideOutput(timeoutTag);
```

### Advanced Pattern Features

```java
// Quantifiers — match one or more consecutive failures
Pattern.<TransactionEvent>begin("failures")
    .where(SimpleCondition.of(e -> e.getStatus().equals("FAILED")))
    .times(3)              // exactly 3
    // .timesOrMore(3)     // 3 or more
    // .oneOrMore()        // 1 or more (greedy by default)
    .within(Time.minutes(10));

// Loop conditions — each event in a sequence checked against previous
Pattern.<TransactionEvent>begin("escalating")
    .where(SimpleCondition.of(e -> e.getAmount() > 100))
    .followedBy("larger")
    .where((event, ctx) -> {
        // Access previously matched events!
        double prevAmount = ctx.getEventsForPattern("escalating")
            .iterator().next().getAmount();
        return event.getAmount() > prevAmount * 1.5;  // 50% larger
    });
```

### SKIP Strategy
When a pattern matches, how far to advance before looking for the next match:
- `NO_SKIP`: Every possible match returned (including overlapping)
- `SKIP_TO_NEXT`: Skip past the first event of the current match
- `SKIP_PAST_LAST_EVENT`: Skip past entire matched sequence (no overlapping)

```java
AfterMatchSkipStrategy skipStrategy = AfterMatchSkipStrategy.skipPastLastEvent();
Pattern<TransactionEvent, ?> pattern = Pattern
    .<TransactionEvent>begin("start", skipStrategy)
    ...;
```
